T2  NAIVE PRICE of a step, held constant over all 109 pre-seam years.
    This is an UPPER BOUND on the seam's cumulative contribution, not an estimate:
    a diagnostic model at a lower CO2 level should have a smaller sink, not one
    offset by a constant. T3 replaces it.
      atm_growth   step +0.239 GtC/yr (0.54 sigma boot)  =>    +26.0 GtC = +12.24 ppm  (+58 % of 44.7 GtC)   -- step NOT resolved
      ocean_sink   step +0.232 GtC/yr (3.18 sigma boot)  =>    +25.3 GtC = +11.93 ppm  (+57 % of 44.7 GtC)

T3  BACKCAST ocean_sink ~ (CO2 - 276.88 ppm), fitted post-seam, evaluated pre-seam.
    CO2 excess: 8.6 ppm at 1850, 38.3 at 1959, 144.9 at 2024.
    This is an EXTRAPOLATION below the fitted range; both windows are reported.
    near (1959-1980)   slope 0.03338 GtC/yr per ppm, R2 0.838 | pre-seam gap: mean +0.062, cum    +6.8 GtC = +3.20 ppm (+15 % of 44.7)
    full (1959-2024)   slope 0.02083 GtC/yr per ppm, R2 0.946 | pre-seam gap: mean +0.475, cum   +51.8 GtC = +24.37 ppm (+116 % of 44.7)
    VERDICT: the two windows disagree by 7.6x (15 % to 116 % of the pre-seam
    cumulative), because the sink-vs-CO2 relation is CURVED (the buffer factor rises
    with CO2) and the pre-seam era is outside the fitted range. The backcast does NOT
    identify the seam's cumulative contribution, and no window is chosen here.

